Which licence covers what
-------------------------

GPL-3.0-or-later (LICENSE)
    The operating system: kernel/, boot/, loader/, shell/, net/, crypto/,
    usb/, web/, media/, apps/, tools/, the Makefile and build.sh, and the
    images, sounds and other files they build from, unless a file or a
    notice below says otherwise.

BSD-2-Clause (sdk/LICENSE, LICENSES/BSD-2-Clause.txt)
    The SDK - sdk/: its headers, libraries, tools and examples - and Banana
    OS's own files in ports/ (the glue between Banana OS and the ported
    programs: launchers, bindings, build scripts). These are what apps link
    against or are built with, so they stay permissive: an app made with
    the SDK may use any licence, open or closed. Linking the SDK into an app
    does not make the app subject to the GPL.

Their own licences (THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES)
    third_party/, the programs ports/ builds (OpenJDK, Mesa, TinyCC, FFmpeg,
    LWJGL), the fonts in fonts/, the Khronos headers in sdk/include/GL and
    sdk/include/KHR, and the test fixtures in web/fixtures and
    web/test/cases.

Each source file says which of the two it is under, on its first line(s):
    SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
    SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
Generated files (wallpaper data, embedded scripts, OpenCL and OpenGL tables)
follow the file they are generated from.


Before the GPL
--------------

Until October 2026 all of Banana OS was under the BSD 2-Clause
licence. That licence allows the code to be distributed under the GPL, as it
is now, provided the BSD copyright notice and conditions are kept: they are,
in LICENSES/BSD-2-Clause.txt. Copies of Banana OS published before the
change remain available under the BSD licence they were published under, and
code contributed by others under the BSD licence keeps that permission for
its own part. New contributions are accepted under the licence of the part
of the tree they change (GPL-3.0-or-later, or BSD-2-Clause for sdk/ and
ports/).
